Attach to a terminal somebody else owns, instead of forking one.

fbterm always calls forkpty() and execs a shell on the fresh slave, which
makes fbterm the parent of the login program. A child inherits its parent's
identity, so on Samsara the getty it execs is a uid 1000 program, and a getty
that cannot setuid(0) refuses every root login -- after already having
accepted the password, so the screen says only "could not set up the
session".

That is not a getty bug: a getty has to start privileged because it becomes
whoever logs in, and nothing unprivileged can start one. So the kernel starts
it and passes the terminal to fbterm with --attach-fd=N. fbterm reads and
writes that descriptor and starts no process of its own, which is the
arrangement a terminal emulator is normally given when told to use a terminal
somebody else opened.

Four things follow from not having made the pty, and each is silent when wrong.
fbterm must take its keyboard from the attached descriptor rather than from
stdin, because stdin is now the end the program on the other side is reading,
and two readers of one descriptor is a race rather than a terminal. It must
leave the terminal's settings alone, because TCSAFLUSH discards input that has
been typed but not yet read -- which silently ate the password. It must not
size itself before it has been sized, since its own dimensions are zero until
the caller resizes it. And it must not reach for forkpty at all, which would
put it back in the position of being the login program's parent.

The option is declared as taking a value, and the value is checked. Declared
without the colon, getopt_long treats `--attach-fd=0` as a flag, leaves `optarg`
NULL, and the atoi faults: the terminal dies before its first message, so there
is nothing on the screen and nothing in the log to say why, and the display
keeps showing the kernel console as though the terminal had never started.

Without the option this is exactly upstream.

Generated with `git diff` against the pinned fbterm commit
419c6be55413a7069fbcc66883d58caa94ba3933. Regenerate with
ports/fbterm/build.sh; never edit by hand.

diff --git a/src/fbconfig.cpp b/src/fbconfig.cpp
index 8249746..e532d79 100644
--- a/src/fbconfig.cpp
+++ b/src/fbconfig.cpp
@@ -37,6 +37,7 @@ Config::Config()
 	mShellCommand = 0;
 	mConfigBuf = 0;
 	mConfigEntrys = 0;
+	mAttachFd = -1;
 
 	const s8 *home = getenv("HOME");
 	if (!home) {
@@ -228,6 +229,8 @@ bool Config::parseArgs(s32 argc, s8 **argv)
 	static const option options[] = {
 		{ "help", no_argument, 0, 'h' },
 		{ "version", no_argument, 0, 'V' },
+		/* Samsara: draw a terminal somebody else owns. See createShellProcess. */
+		{ "attach-fd", required_argument, 0, 'A' },
 		{ "verbose", no_argument, 0, 'v' },
 		{ "font-names", required_argument, 0, 'n' },
 		{ "font-size", required_argument, 0, 's' },
@@ -250,8 +253,22 @@ bool Config::parseArgs(s32 argc, s8 **argv)
 	};
 
 	s32 index;
-	while ((index = getopt_long(argc, argv, "Vvhn:s:f:b:e:r:i:c:C:W:H:B:al", options, 0)) != -1) {
+	while ((index = getopt_long(argc, argv, "VA:vn:s:f:b:e:r:i:c:C:W:H:B:al", options, 0)) != -1) {
 		switch (index) {
+		case 'A':
+			/* An option that takes a value and arrives without one has
+			 * to be an error, not a dereference of NULL: getopt_long
+			 * leaves optarg NULL in that case and the atoi faults.
+			 * The terminal then dies before its first message, so there
+			 * is nothing on the screen and nothing in the log to say why.
+			 */
+			if (!optarg) {
+				fprintf(stderr, "fbterm: --attach-fd needs a descriptor\n");
+				return false;
+			}
+			mAttachFd = atoi(optarg);
+			break;
+
 		case 'V':
 			printf("FbTerm version " VERSION "\n");
 			return false;
